    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25d-perchlike-fishes-4-south-american-dwarf-cichlids/apistogramma_gephyra_bred_en/",
      "html": "<p>Apistogramma gephyra and A. agassizii are really difficult to tell apart. They also appear at least in some places together in the natural habitat, so the origin is of no help. Both species never hybridize in the wild, which is the best evidence that they represent two different species.</p><p>The best way to tell the two species apart is to look at the dorsal fin of adult males. In A. gephyra there is a red seam in that fin, which hardly ever appears in A. agassizii males.</p><p>Fully grown German-bred specimens of A. gephyra, bred by an enthusiast who is deeply involved with Apistogramma, are available at 4-8 cm. These fish give the lie to the often heard sentence \"Apistogramma gephyra looks like a colourless A. agassizii\": they are really beautiful fishes.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25d-perchlike-fishes-4-south-american-dwarf-cichlids/apistogramma_gephyra_bred_en/",
      "html": "<p>Keeping Apistogramma gephyra is problem-free. Like all Apistogramma species, A. gephyra needs a gravel in the tank that is composed at least in some places of fine sand.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25d-perchlike-fishes-4-south-american-dwarf-cichlids/apistogramma_gephyra_bred_en/",
      "html": "<p>Only for breeding purposes it is often necessary to use very soft, acidic water.</p>"
    }
